  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA surface stationary boundary lingers over the lower Ohio\nValley, though forcing in the 850-700 mb layer extends well\nnorth of this boundary. In the immediate near term, light to \nmoderate rain showers have generally remained south of a Wood to\nWayne County line. Showers should gradually expand northward to\nas far as a Toledo-Lorain-Akron line but the risk for heavy rain\nis likely to rain towards our southwest counties (Marion to\nKnox) where the latest HRRR cycles project an inch of rain. The\nrisk for >2\" of rain and subsequent flooding is low but not\ncompletely out of the question, so we'll have to monitor for\nthat this morning. Rain showers dissipate through the afternoon\nand we should be completely rain free by around 21Z.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe next period for unsettled weather will be Saturday night \nthrough Sunday as a low pressure system moves east across the \nGreat Lakes.\n\nA stationary front lingering over the Ohio Valley will lift\nnorthward across the area Saturday night in response to low\npressure tracking across the Great Lakes region. A period of \nheavy rain is becoming more likely Saturday night as a highly-\nanomalous moist air mass, characterized by PWATS exceeding 2.0 \ninches, which is approaching the climatological maximum, enters\nthe area. There is a slight risk (level 2 of 4) for excessive\nrainfall and a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) for severe weather \nSaturday night.\n\nThe latest model guidance continues to be trending the strong \nto severe storm threat further south of the area as any \nredevelopment in the afternoon will likely focus along the \nresidual boundary from the overnight convection.",
